# MSc AI Capstone Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Context
This is a MSc Artificial Intelligence capstone project focused on developing domain-specific AI for educational syllabus generation.

### Core Problem
Current LLMs can generate text that looks like a syllabus but lack specific educational structure and pedagogical understanding for coherent, educationally sound course content.

### Solution Approach
Building a domain-specific AI model that:
- Takes minimal inputs (course description, learning objectives, problem statements)
- Generates structured, coherent course syllabi
- Understands educational frameworks and learning progression
- Maintains pedagogical quality throughout content

### Technical Innovation
- Custom neural architecture for educational content structure
- Specialized word embeddings trained on educational terminology
- Sequence-to-sequence modeling with attention mechanisms
- Training data from Open Syllabus Project API and educational resources

## Project Structure

### Directory Organization
- `docs/` - Academic documentation, literature review, methodology, progress reports
- `research/` - Literature collection, analysis, datasets research
- `src/` - Source code (data processing, models, training, evaluation, utilities)
- `experiments/` - Experimental code, baseline models, custom architectures
- `data/` - Raw, processed, and synthetic datasets
- `notebooks/` - Jupyter notebooks for exploration and prototyping
- `Notes/` - Course notes and existing project documents

### Key Files
- `README.md` - Main project overview and setup instructions
- `requirements.txt` - Python dependencies (minimal, add as needed)
- `environment.yml` - Conda environment specification
- `.gitignore` - Version control exclusions

## Coding Standards & Preferences

### Python Style
- Follow PEP 8 standards
- Use type hints for functions and classes
- Docstrings for all public functions using Google style
- Prefer descriptive variable names over comments
- Maximum line length: 88 characters (Black formatter compatible)

### AI/ML Specific Guidelines
- Use PyTorch as primary deep learning framework
- Implement reproducible experiments with seed setting
- Log experiments with clear naming conventions
- Separate data processing, model definition, training, and evaluation
- Include evaluation metrics in all model experiments
- Document hyperparameters and model architectures

### Documentation Standards
- All major functions and classes must have docstrings
- README files in each major directory explaining purpose
- Code comments for complex algorithms or domain-specific logic
- Academic citations in docstrings when implementing published methods

### File Naming Conventions
- Python files: lowercase with underscores (snake_case)
- Classes: PascalCase
- Functions and variables: snake_case
- Constants: UPPER_CASE
- Notebooks: descriptive names with dates for experiments

### Dependencies Management
- Add dependencies to requirements.txt only when needed
- Pin versions for reproducibility in final implementation
- Use virtual environments for development
- Document any system dependencies in README

## Academic Project Considerations

### Research Phase (Current: Weeks 1-8)
- Focus on literature review and methodology
- Document all research decisions and rationale
- Maintain bibliography with proper citations
- Track research gaps and opportunities

### Implementation Phases
- Start with baseline models before custom architectures
- Implement comprehensive evaluation metrics early
- Regular experimental logging and results documentation
- Iterative development with clear version control

### Evaluation Focus
- Implement both technical metrics (ROUGE, BERTScore) and educational quality assessments
- Bloom's taxonomy alignment analysis
- Pedagogical framework adherence
- Compare against general-purpose LLM baselines

## AI Assistant Guidelines

### When Helping with Code
- Prioritize educational domain knowledge when relevant
- Suggest appropriate evaluation metrics for educational content
- Consider pedagogical implications of design decisions
- Reference relevant educational technology research when applicable

### When Helping with Research
- Suggest relevant academic databases and search terms
- Help identify key papers in AI-education intersection
- Assist with methodology design for educational content evaluation
- Provide insights on experimental design for academic rigor

### When Helping with Documentation
- Maintain academic writing standards
- Include proper citations and references
- Structure documentation for academic audience
- Balance technical detail with accessibility

### Dependencies and Tools
- Only suggest adding dependencies when clearly beneficial
- Prefer well-established libraries with good documentation
- Consider computational requirements for academic environment
- Suggest tools that aid in research reproducibility

### Progress Tracking Support
- Help maintain consistent documentation patterns
- Suggest improvements to project organization
- Assist with milestone tracking and goal setting
- Support academic timeline management

## Project Phase Awareness
Current phase information should be maintained in separate progress tracking files. AI should:
- Consider current project phase when making suggestions
- Prioritize tasks appropriate to current timeline
- Suggest phase-appropriate tools and approaches
- Help maintain academic project momentum

## Quality Standards
- All code should be production-ready quality even in research phase
- Experiments should be reproducible
- Documentation should meet academic standards
- Results should be clearly presented and analyzed

Remember: This is an academic research project contributing to AI-education field. Balance innovation with rigorous academic standards. 